The impact of asynchronous updates on the scalability of federated learning is well-documented (Leslie et al., 2020). However, the core challenge of data heterogeneity—where the target global model must account for diverse local data distributions—remains a primary obstacle (Yang et al., 2019). While many existing approaches attempt to address this, their analyses often depend on the assumption of bounded gradient norms. We demonstrate how to remove this restriction while maintaining polynomial complexity relative to the average norm of local gradients, thereby accommodating datasets with diverse generators and labels. This removal of the boundedness assumption represents a distinct contribution of our work.

Our main theoretical results establish a convergence rate for \textit{FedBuff} in terms of the average norm of local gradients, drawing parallels to mini-batch SGD analysis (Bottou et al., 2018). This result is significant because it circumvents the need for bounding the norm of local gradients, a requirement that is difficult to satisfy in general settings, especially when generators are unknown in black-box scenarios. Additionally, we compare \textit{FedBuff} with other asynchronous algorithms and conclude with suggestions for future research.

