 widely adopted method for generating recommendations, including those from relevant websites, due to its ability to forecast user preferences by analyzing their similarities with other users. However, CF faces the well-documented cold-start problem, which hinders performance when new items or users are introduced. This issue arises because traditional CF depends heavily on historical user-item interaction data to build associations. To overcome these limitations, various machine learning techniques have been applied to enhance CF, with the primary objective of increasing the diversity of suggested items and enriching users' item collections. This paper introduces a collaborative filtering approach integrated with K-means clustering to address these specific challenges. The method involves clusing similar users into groups before applying CF to predict each user's preferences. This strategy expands the number of relevant items recommended to every user. The proposed method, CF with K-means clustering, is evaluated against standard CF across two real-world datasets: Amazon books (books-fm) and Yelp datasets (reviews-fm).CF with K-means clustering demonstrates improved performance over conventional CF, evidenced by higher item diversity in user recommendations. Specifically, the approach yields an 11% increase in the count of unique items recommended on the Amazon books dataset and an 18% increase on the Yelp dataset. Furthermore, the method effectively mitigates cold-start issues for new items.CF with K-means clustering is accessible for download at: https://github.com/aryan-kaushal/CF_with_Kmeans_cluster.

